A small inheritance hierarchy and its UML diagram should be a direct, boring translation — if they don't match, one of them is wrong.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;
    double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}
class Rectangle extends Shape {
    double width, height;
    double area() { return width * height; }
}
```

The UML equivalent:

```
         Shape (abstract)
        △ + area(): double
        |
   ┌────┴─────┐
Circle      Rectangle
```

Each `extends` becomes a hollow-triangle inheritance arrow pointing at `Shape`, and the abstract method shows up once, in the parent — subclasses don't redeclare it in the diagram just because they override it in code.
